# An unboxing conversion goes by the type of the boxed value.
# A value that is not an int32 is read as a double, and a value that is not a number is taken for the first marker of the type, both without a check.
# With a false proof that goes wrong silently. With the proof-checking switches those two branches check.
# Here the guard of f1 unboxes its operand for a Body that takes a Double, one operation after its own test, so no check in the Body can see what arrived.
# Fault injection, testing=UnderstateEffects:Callee/f1/TestsOfGuard: the emitter lets every test of the guard of f1 pass.
# f1 = sum
# RUN: plan units mechanisms=all
# CHECK: unit f1:Body (Double)
# CHECK: unit f1:ParameterGuard (JSValue) -> (JSValue) tests (number)
# RUN(RIGHT): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime
# RIGHT: 40 -1
# RIGHT-NEXT: 0NaNabNaNabNaNabNaNab
# RIGHT-NEXT: went on
# RIGHT-NEXT: exited with 0
# RUN(ENTRIES): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtEntries,UnderstateEffects:Callee/f1/TestsOfGuard
# ENTRIES: 40 -1
# ENTRIES: found string in the result of an operation
# ENTRIES-NOT: went on
# ENTRIES: crashed
# RUN(PROOFS): run-in-shell mechanisms=all testing=CheckProofsAtRunTime,UnderstateEffects:Callee/f1/TestsOfGuard
# PROOFS: 40 -1
# PROOFS: found string in the result of an operation
# PROOFS-NOT: went on
# PROOFS: crashed
